<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="selectors" title="Selectors">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        A selector is the text that names a member: the <code>method</code> element of an
        injection, the <code>target</code> of an <code>@At</code>. It constrains only the parts
        you spell out. Matching runs against one class at a time.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["method = charge(BigDecimal)"] --> B["Owner, name, parameters, return type"]
    B --> C{"How many declared methods fit?"}
    C -->|none| D["AW1020, with every candidate listed"]
    C -->|one| E["That overload is woven"]
    C -->|several| F["AW1021, with the matches listed"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="What each part constrains" id="parts">
        <p>The name is the only part you cannot leave off.</p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Part</td>
                <td>With it</td>
                <td>Without it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Owner</td>
                <td>only that class</td>
                <td>any class</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Parameter list</td>
                <td>that arity, then position by position</td>
                <td>any signature</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Each parameter type</td>
                <td>that erased type</td>
                <td><code>*</code> matches any</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Return or field type</td>
                <td>that erased type</td>
                <td>any type</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <list>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <code>*</code> stands for a whole name, never part of one.
                    <code>get*</code> is <code>AW1015</code>; use <code>*</code> alone, or name
                    the method in full.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Type arguments parse, then get thrown away. A class file records erased
                    signatures, and <code>AW1016</code> says so; delete them.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <a href="selector-grammar.topic">The selector grammar</a> has the rest, including the
                    constant forms an <a href="injection-points.topic">injection point</a> takes.
                </p>
            </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Absent, empty and wildcard" id="arity">
        <p>Three spellings of one name behave differently, and the difference is the feature.</p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="charge">
                <p>
                    Every method of that name, whatever it takes and returns. Write it when the
                    target has one <code>charge</code> and its signature may still move.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="charge()">
                <p>
                    Only the overload taking nothing. The parentheses have to close up.
                    <code>charge( )</code> is <code>AW1015</code>: a type is expected once
                    anything but <code>)</code> follows. Remove the space.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="*">
                <p>
                    Every method the target declares. At compile time, matching several is
                    <code>AW1022</code> unless <code>allow</code> is set above zero — it says
                    "several is intended", not how many; a count that later grows is not caught.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                <code>allow</code> is checked again when the class is woven, and differently:
                there it is a ceiling, and a wildcard matching more methods than <code>allow</code>
                permits is <code>AW1044</code>. See <a href="pipeline.topic">the pipeline</a> for
                that check.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Two spellings, one selector" id="forms">
        <p>
            One member, two ways to name it. Which one you write decides whether the text is a
            pattern or a pin.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="text">
charge(BigDecimal)
desc:com/acme/Gateway.charge(Ljava/math/BigDecimal;)V
        </code-block>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Source form">
                <p>
                    The one to write by hand. It names types the way Java source does, and only
                    this form takes a wildcard. Slashes belong to the other one.
                    <code>com/acme/Gateway.charge()</code> is <code>AW1015</code>: use dots, or
                    add the <code>desc:</code> prefix.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Descriptor form">
                <p>
                    The one to paste from <code>javap -s</code>. Every type in
                    it is exact. It admits no wildcard and names one member. Its return type is
                    part of what it names, so
                    <code>desc:charge(Ljava/math/BigDecimal;)</code> is <code>AW1019</code>; add
                    <code>V</code> for <code>void</code>.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            Descriptor syntax without the prefix is <code>AW1017</code>. It is reported only after
            the source reading has failed, and it carries a corrected spelling. Use that.
            <a href="selector-grammar.topic">The selector grammar</a> gives both forms in full.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where inheritance stops" id="candidates">
        <p>
            Candidates come from the target itself, never from what it inherits. Name the class
            that declares the member, and weave there.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Against a target still in source, the processor reads its declared methods; a
                    constructor is never among them, so <code>&lt;init&gt;()</code> is
                    <code>AW1020</code>. At weave time the engine reads the class file itself, and
                    everything it holds counts: constructors as <code>&lt;init&gt;</code>, the
                    static initialiser as <code>&lt;clinit&gt;</code>, and the bridge and synthetic
                    methods a compiler emitted.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A bare name can therefore match a compiler-generated method alongside the one
                    you meant. That is <code>AW1021</code>, not <code>AW1020</code> — something
                    matched, more than one thing. <code>AW1021</code> prints what it matched;
                    add the parameter types, or pin one with <code>desc:</code>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Which classes a weave claims is a separate question.
                    <a href="weaving-model.topic">The weaving model</a> settles that, and
                    <a href="pipeline.topic">the pipeline</a> has the order.
                </p>
            </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Read twice, compared differently" id="checked-twice">
        <p>
            The annotation processor checks your selector, and the engine checks it again. They do
            not compare the same things.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>What it states</td>
                <td>At compile time</td>
                <td>When the class is woven</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A return type</td>
                <td>not compared</td>
                <td>compared, if parsed as one</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Parameter wildcard</td>
                <td>no match</td>
                <td>matches any type</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A qualified owner</td>
                <td>must be on the classpath</td>
                <td>must be the woven class</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A wildcard, many hits</td>
                <td>several needs allow set</td>
                <td>allow is the ceiling</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            A qualified owner missing from the compile classpath is <code>AW1010</code>. Check the
            spelling, or drop the owner when the member sits on the weave's own target. A simple
            name is never reported that way, since resolving one needs imports no class file
            records.
        </p>
    </chapter>

